;
THE LOCAL CONSOLE AND BYE

A - At the sysop's console
B - A word with the caller

:A
AT THE SYSOP'S CONSOLE

When a caller is online, your local keyboard and his remote keyboard
ordinarily feed the same computer. Don't start a disk operation while someone
is composing a message.

Use the following local BYE controls in shared mode:

  Key - Action
  Ctrl-B - Blank the caller and enter private operation; again to unblank
  Ctrl-G - Toggle the page bell and paging availability
  Ctrl-Q - Enter sysop message mode
  Ctrl-X - Leave message mode
  Ctrl-N - Force caller hangup

While blanked, most local control keys go to the application rather than BYE,
except Ctrl-B. Finish private work, leave the application and return to A0:
before unblanking. With a tmux wrapper, Ctrl-B Ctrl-B sends a literal Ctrl-B
to the guest; the distributed launcher needs only one.

Between calls, local Ctrl-C opens BYE's menu. Select J to jump to CP/M with
BYE attached if you intend to use AUTH. RETURN instead enters ordinary,
unpatched CP/M; AUTH needs the resident interface, so don't confuse the two
choices.

:B
A WORD WITH THE CALLER

When paged by CHAT, answer with Escape on the local console. Both ends need an
80-column, 24-row ANSI/VT100 terminal for the split display. Your text appears
above the caller's; the panes wrap and scroll separately. Backspace erases
within the current line, Tab inserts spaces, and Ctrl-R redraws. Ctrl-C or
Ctrl-X ends chat. CHAT /S provides the plain scrolling alternative.

A caller in RBBS must return to CP/M with C before invoking CHAT. Ctrl-G at
the local console toggles paging availability. If you need to attend to
private business, local Ctrl-B ends split chat and blanks the caller.
